WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:02.860
Film Courage: Do beat sheets work?

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:05.520
Jill Chamberlain, Script Consultant/Author/Writer:
I think having an outline is an excellent

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.250
idea.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.670
I do know writers who don’t need them.

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:15.520
I think they need something and these are
going to be…I speak of writers who I have

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:21.560
worked with so they tend to already be devotees
of using The Nutshell Technique.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.220
And that might be all they do.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.730
They won’t do each sheet, they’ll just
do The Nutshell Technique form.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:30.420
And as long as they are true to that they’ll
have it and it’s nice and solid and maybe

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:35.140
they will work with me one-on-one and make
sure their Nutshell is solid, they will tack

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:39.010
it up on their bulletin board and they will
write from that and they don’t do any other

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:41.480
beat sheet or outline.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:46.090
I think most writers need a beat sheet or
outline, they need more detail than that.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:55.630
But as long as you have a solid structure
you don’t necessarily need more than this.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:01:03.410
This isn’t drawn to scale but you will see
that most of my structure is all in Act 1

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:08.560
and Act 3 and the only thing that is in A
2 is this one element here and Act 2 is actually

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.850
twice as long.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:14.930
So I don’t have a bunch of structure requirements,
they don’t have the required beats in your

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:18.720
Act 2 even thought Act 2 is twice as long.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:25.140
I strongly believe that if you properly structure
things in Act 1 and properly structure things

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.820
in Act 3 it does all of the heavy lifting
of supporting your Act 2 even though your

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.900
Act 2 is twice as long.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:35.049
As long as you maintain conflict.

00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:41.229
So I know lots of writers who hate beat sheets
and ask me “Can I really throw it out?”

00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:42.229
You absolutely can.

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:44.610
If you don’t like doing a beat sheet, if
you don’t find that helpful you don’t

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:45.610
have to.

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:50.800
As long as you find a nice solid structure
and you stay true to it this should be enough

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.479
to guide you as long as you have conflict.

00:01:53.479 --> 00:02:01.780
I do think most writers need more detail but
it’s up to the writer a little bit.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:06.590
More detail…but I don’t think it’s a
good idea to do super detailed one because

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:09.599
you want to allow a certain amount of discovery
on the page.

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:16.029
That’s why I like having the looser structure
of The Nutshell to begin with.

00:02:16.029 --> 00:02:19.329
We have a couple of things you’ve got to
hit.

00:02:19.329 --> 00:02:23.430
If you shift from them then you are screwing
up your story.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:29.029
But if you stay solid to these elements that
might be all that you need and you can just

00:02:29.029 --> 00:02:35.349
write and live on the page as long as you
hit those couple of moments.

00:02:35.349 --> 00:02:39.370
Perhaps you need a little bit more structure,
a little bit more of an outline or a beat

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:44.580
sheet but I don’t recommend doing super-detailed
ones.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:50.300
There is a thing I call “beat sheet fatigue”
where people just spent too much time on that

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:55.560
particularly if you are getting feedback or
you are sharing your Nutshell or (I’m sorry)

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.530
your beat sheet or outline with your writing
group or people like that.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:04.319
Maybe get feedback on like an 8-page one.

00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:08.769
If it’s any longer than that it’s getting
into too so much detail that we’d rather

00:03:08.769 --> 00:03:11.239
read the script at that point.

00:03:11.239 --> 00:03:14.819
A beat sheet is ultimately for your benefit
anyway.

00:03:14.819 --> 00:03:16.790
It’s for whatever you need.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:21.060
So if you need a little more structure than
just this and like I said I think most people

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:26.849
need a little bit more I think it’s a good
idea to have a loose guide of this happens

00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:28.079
and then this happens and then this happens.

00:03:28.079 --> 00:03:31.870
But kind of loose so that you can discover
somewhat on the page.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.730
Film Courage: What about conflict fatigue?

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:39.989
What about someone adding too much conflict
right away but there is no real purpose?

00:03:39.989 --> 00:03:46.040
It’s just creating friction because the
writer thinks they need it but then it doesn’t

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:47.370
really lend itself to the story?

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:48.370
Do you see this a lot?

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:50.120
Or do you see not enough conflict?

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:56.669
Jill: I see not enough conflict more often
as a problem.

00:03:56.669 --> 00:04:02.220
What you are describing of there being too
much it sounds like to me it would be the

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:10.469
case of somebody who hadn’t really figured
out what the story is and they just know Screenwriter

00:04:10.469 --> 00:04:12.049
101 I’m supposed to have conflict right?

00:04:12.049 --> 00:04:15.239
So they just have a bunch of conflict but
they don’t really have purpose.

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:21.290
So it sounds like it might be a little situational
or episodic but I’d have to see this on

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:23.090
a case-by-case basis.

